{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues the panel outperforms the expert, but the problem asks for an argument for OR against the claim; the target only argues for it, omitting the against case. However, the real fault is in the evidence criterion: it claims calibration on counterintuitive cases proves genuine gain, but if the checklist dictates the answer on those cases, high confidence when right is still mere compliance with a rule that happens to be correct — the target does not show what distinguishes the panel genuinely monitoring rule correctness versus compliantly following a correct rule. The countercondition about high box-ticking on counterintuitive cases is conceded, which dissolves the distinguishing power the target claims to provide.","target":"245a2a4949e7026b673342c903ca86b494db523fd0dbcba49abfb9fc4603cadf"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's evidence criterion requires showing the panel's accuracy is insensitive to checklist corruption but sensitive to aggregation method within a single evaluation. A single evaluation cannot show insensitivity to corruption unless it includes a corrupted-checklist arm, but then the panel is no longer following the explicit checklist specified by the problem — it is a different intervention. The target thus cannot distinguish genuine checklist-mediated gain from aggregation gain using the evaluation it describes, because detecting insensitivity to corruption requires comparing across checklist conditions, which a single evaluation of the stated intervention does not provide. The countercondition conceding that errors may be correlated (e.g., all follow the same wrong rule) further undermines the independence assumption on which the entire wisdom-of-crowds mechanism depends.","target":"2be6f60d911d4453d0ed5b71ad47674993c13b60e58ea5a536b14f463ce12ad6"}]}